> projects or becoming glorified engineers for same.

Speaking of which, Nick and I just last week were talking (in person,
no less) about the Supercollider and the fact, which I take to be in
support of my "physics is slowing down" point, that the $10 billion
proposed (now cancelled) expenditure will produce essentially no new
physics--barring some surprise--and will mainly just establish the
mass of the Higgs boson. (Note: a truly astounding surprise _could_
come out of the Supercollider, and this could make it worthwhile. Such
a surprise is unlikely. If this reasoning sounds fishy to you, it is
based on a Bayesian outlook: past accelerators have only occasionally
produced true surprises, the J/psi being one of them, and as energies
get greater, the surprises get sparser....we probably need better than log
increases in accelerator energy to see even as many surprises per unit
time as we've seen in the past.)

> One of the lessons of evolution is "punctuated equilibrium" --
> periods of stasis or slow growth punctuated by periods of ferment
> as pathways to new fertile areas are opened.  Even if we've entered
> a period of stasis, that doesn't tell us that we've explored most
> of the search space.

Yes, I've argued this in my "k-space" models many years (the knowledge
space, the "mine" and "ore" metaphors, the creation of new fields.

....
> No, you argued that there is less that needs to be explained now than
> at the turn of the century.  I argue that the opposite is true.
> Of course statistics would be better than anecdotes, but are hard
> to come by.

And I stick to that. The world today seems much more understandable to
me than I suspect it did to most folks in 1900. The anecdote about the
proposal to shut down the Patent Office because everything had already
been invented turns out to be misleading.

Science has worked, and we now have fairly good theories of most
everything around us. In some areas related to "complex systems,"
notably biology, social systems, economics, etc., there are large gaps
in our knowledge. But these kinds of gaps are of a different sort and
may be implicit in such evolved, emergent systems.

> > Of course not! People can be, and are, just as smart as before. But
> > the late 19th and early 20th century happened (we could debated the
> > economic and sociological reasons, the links with markets, the
> > expansion of industry, etc.) to be a time of major exploration of
> > previously unexplored parts  of knowledge space. 
> 
> Let's debate them.  Why 19th and early 20th?  Why not 18th or 
> 10th or 3rd BC?  Why not mid and late 20th?  Why not 21st century?  

Many reasons why the late 19th, early 20th century was so successful:

- relative peace, after generations of civil and national wars
throughout Europe
- relative prosperity, resulting in a flourishing of academia
- the Industrial Revolution, with new tools, new instruments
(telescopes, spectrometers, interferometers, etc.)
- emergence of the modern form of mathematics, with analysis, logic,
differential geometry, set theory all reaching fruition (and modern
form) in this same period 
- synergism between compelling (and essentially complete) theories of
statistical mechanics, electromagnetism, and quantum mechanics....all
of these were formulated in the 1880-1900 period in essentially their
modern form (QM needed another 30 years to be adequately completed).


> "There you go again!".  This covers 110 years, from 1840s (Darwin) to 1940s
> (communications & computer theory), compared to 50 years of
> theory since then, the consequences of which are harder to determine

Fine, I will willingly throw out the "earliest" and "latest" examples.
My point was that the 30 years from around 1880 to around 1910
produced our modern view of things, from the fields named above to
the underpinning of our machinery...our light bulbs, our electric
motors, our telephones, and even our computers (Babbage, Boole, etc.).

I suspect these were the concepts/basis vectors that were _there to be
found_. Later work, on quarks for example, did not (and very likely
will not, in the next few hundred years) produce the same cornucopia
of resulting derivavite theories and technologies. Science fiction
writers may be enamored of "gravitonic" and perhaps even "quarktronic"
devices, but nearly everything you see around you comes from the basic
work in thermodynamics, electromagnetic theory, and quantum mechanics.

> > I think it was Rutherford who said "All science is either physics or
> > stamp collecting." While overly dismissive, and probably not even
> > fully believed by him, there's a kernel of truth there. 
> 
> Computers are superb at stamp collecting, and will soon be quite
> good at physics.  Already they're very good at doing the math
> of physics, simulations, statistical regressions, etc. if set up
> properly; GP adds the capability of discovering new models.
> In most cases software is orders of magnitude faster than
> human scientists, and can take advantage of the ongoing increases
> in computer speed.

There may be some promising indications of success, but for now I
think the verdict is still out on this. Personally, I have faith in
GP--and the last thing I want is for this debate between Nick and me
to be taken as evidence that I am "against" GP techniques....far from
it. But I have not seen strong evidence that Koza or Rice or anyone
else is on the verge of doing new physics.

The "discovery" of Kepler's laws through regression against
combinations of symbolic primitives (sines and cosines, squares, etc.)
is not all that stunning. It's a kind of data-fitting that in fact
Kepler did.

Other previously trendy systems, such as Doug Lenat's famed "Eurisko"
system, were alleged to have discovered new mathematical theorems. So
what became of them? Why aren't they being used today?

I've written too much already and so will stop here.

Unless there is evidence of interest by others, perhasp Nick and I
should take this to e-mail or in-person meetings...

--Tim May


