Date: Thu, 4 Nov 93 1:03:19 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: No New Continents (was: Future Science)

Paul Whitmore writes:

> open up the aperture since the topic is quite fruitful. perhaps it is 
...
> 
> i have not been too persuaded on the likelihood that the knowledge space 
> is rather well-charted

Let me summarize my point about the rate of progress in science in
another way, by using a metaphor: geography and the discovery of the
major continents.

There was a time of course when the nature of the continents--their
existence, their location, their geology, etc.--was not known to "teh
civilized world." Expeditions were sent out, the New World was
incorrectly identified as Asia (India), and so on.

A period of intense exploration, lasting from around 1400 to around
1600 essentially charted the main continents. By 1700 or so maps were
pretty much in their current form, with the "most significant bits"
(in the AIT terms we've been using lately) identified, the coarse
features.

Better navigation instruments, better map-making methods, and lots of
detailed work certainly made the maps better, occasionally even
changing them fairly radically. But the basic features had already
been identified.

Modern LANDSAT and SPOT satellites, precision surveys, and detailed
topographic surveys have mapped the Earth's surface to an accuracy of
a few tens of meters in most places.

And though the raw number of bits about the geography of the Earth is
rising exponentially (zillions of images, gigabytes of data per
satellite pass, cross-indexing of atlases), clearly the "basic
knowledge" of the Earth's geography is not increasing exponentially.
The information content of gigabytes of satellite imagery is not so
high.

No new continents will be discovered no matter how long we look. The
"field of geography" is slowing down, never mind that there are
probably more geographers working for just the National Geographic
Service as there were in the entire world for the 1400-1600 period.

But is science at all like this geography metaphor? That's the
interesting question, isn't it?

I believe that mathematics, for example, still has "new continents" to
discover. Some are not even this optimistic, noting that it has been
many years since brand new fields fo mathematics were discovered.
(There are some issues of semantics as to what constitutes a new
field.) Mathematics may be much richer than many other fields. 

Physics, my former field, shows many signs of having few continents
left undiscovered.

Back to the metaphor: even the undiscovered continents gave evidence
of being there, through rumors about a vast land out past the dragons,
or a huge void in the map, or fragmentary reports from failed
expeditions. Likewise, there were many signs that _something_ was
needed to explain effects we later explained by various applications
of quantum mechanics.

These "continents" are the "basis vectors" I have talked about before,
the building blocks and core ideas. 

I am prepared to admit that there _could_ be all kinds of bizarre,
exotic new physics out there someplace. Maybe superstrings, cosmic
strings, 32-dimensioned hyperbolic spaces, physics inside event
horizons, etc. But there is no immediate evidence for this.

And many of the putatively unexplained things in physics today, such
as the behavior of the new high-temp (30-70 K) superconductors, are
not necessarily evidence that "new physics" is to be found (new
continents, or even new major landforms). Many ensembles of things
will have hard to predict forms and behaviors...that's why molecular
modeling and visualization is so important. But I wouldn't call this
new physics, except in the applied physics (= engineering) sense.

As tools get better, as instruments get more precise, we'll map out
physics to an ever-finer detail, filling in some gaps and occasionally
identifying new landforms (mountain ranges, forests, rivers) that we
missed in our initial survey.

But I doubt this more detailed survey will find many new continents.

(If there is to be found new physics per se, as opposed to discoveries
in systems/emergent behavior/chaos/etc.. then I suspect it'll come out
of astronomical observations. Black holes, cosmic strings, etc. This
is one reason I'm utterly bored with the Supercollider but very
excited about the Keck telescope(s) and their cousins.)

I just wanted to get this explanatory metaphor out there on the table,
or at least on the continental shelf, if you get my drift.

