  Could someone summarize many of the great unexplained fundamental
problems in physics today? Tim's looking for dragons, there's probably
a few. Here's some off the top of my head:

  * prediction of the masses of all the fundamental particles derived from
    simpler principles

  * prediction of how gravity works on the microscopic level
    (despite the fact that the effects might be insignificant, I still
     regard physics as incomplete when we have a discontinous function which
     says "these rules work if our energy is greater than X" or 
     "our scale is bigger than Y") This is my biggest concern with
     GP science. It's possible that a GP could do reasonably well predicting
     solutions based on special intervals and conditions, but would do very
     badly when nearing extremes of the domain.

  * resolve the issue of what happens at singularities

  * figuring out why the universe acts so strange (quantum mechanics)
    is there a more fundamental theory which explains the randomness?

  * possibility of unified theory, all of physics condensed into one
    mathematical representation
    (Barkeley from Star Trek's the Nth Degree "I perceive the universe as
     a single equation and it is SO simple!")

  * other forces, previously unseen, which come into play at extremes?
    
  * what is the structure of space-time, continuous or discrete/foamy?

  * Can causality be violated, and if so, what happens to the universe?

  * universe: open, closed?
 
  * are electrons concious? (just kidding!)


  Most interesting to me is FTL signaling and whether it's possible. The
math says it's possible with the right stuff, but we need a physical theory
capable of ruling out the existence of the special stuff, or we need revised
theories that close the loopholes.

  For many people, the search for FTL is like squaring the circle. I doubt
people will ever stop searching until it is conclusively disproved. 
(Merely quoting relativity isn't good enough) And some no doubt, will
continue searching after it is disproved.

