I was transported by Tim's presentation of the metaphor of continents. 
The image invites (hoary?) fractal-talk; Mandelbrot refers to the 
coastlines as one physical measurement that is open to endless 
re-valuation. I am not sure whether fractals are compressible in AIT. 
They are but one example of the sort of information and structure that 
is left for the future science.

A piece of history from a truly beautiful atlas [Georges Bank, MIT 
Press: 1987]:

Consider the length of time, and the number of mistakes, invested to 
chart the geographic contours of the Georges Bank, "a 150-km-wide and 
280-km wide topographic high that rises more than 100m above the Gulf of 
Maine." (p. 25). this first appeared on a map in 1524. Its shape was 
idealized to a boomerang form in a map of 1537, and this was copied for 
the next 30 years, "even by Mercator on his monumental and momentous 
world chart of 1569." (p 10) The Dutch introduced a style of "sweeping 
curves, graceful ellipses, and crisply pointed cusps" that kept a very 
curlicued shape in circulation from 1650 to 1750. It was not until 1720 
that a map managed to come close to the actual shape of the bank. 

There is a parallel between the dutch linear models, and those currently 
used in most physical theories. 

If physical objects require "harsh experience and hard survey," and if 
their simple sketching reveals a history of cumulative and repetitive 
error, what about more complicated interactions? i will post another 
thread on where life and science might go after physics is plumbed.

