Date: Thu, 04 Nov 93 06:52:56 EST
From: wit@MIT.EDU (paul whitmore)
Subject: Future Science (Biology Uber Physics)

If I were to pick the place where the hard work lies ahead, it seems 
clear that everything becomes vastly more complicated if physical laws 
must include relational models of explanation. Biology and Cognition, 
[also Ecology and Economics], are fields that are far from being 
_outlined_, in the sense that we could even characterize what is NOT 
alive, does NOT count as a case of perception, etc. These problems, if 
they are special, will involve laws that are independent of all the 
physical phenomena that are currently well understood. wormholes and 
weird stuff out in space are even less relevant to these HARD questions.

I am interested in astrophysics, blackholes and quantum strangeness. But 
last yr, I attended a conference where a particle physicist made the 
argument that quantum mechanics basically shielded observers from most 
of the things that go on below the atomic level. Since excitation levels 
need to be highly energetic to change the system's behavior, there is a 
sense in which it doesnt matter what is happening down there. [i am 
presenting a  coarse sketch here of the claim, but it should suffice for 
my next statement]:

We may need to jump in the ring and argue about reductionism. I've read 
excellent arguments for absolutely throwing out the physical composition 
of many interesting systems, and studying the formal arrangements (of 
whatevers) for law-like patterns of behavior. (Jerry Fodor and Zenon 
Pylyshyn have made this argument in terms of cognitive systems). Since 
these are instantiated only in physical systems, whatever lasting 
results can be gleaned from this line of inquiry should ultimately be 
treated with the esteem we currently hold for physics, rather than the 
contempt reserved for stamp-collecting.

I have been hoping to propose a virtual seminar on a pithy and 
revolutionary book: _Life Itself_, by Robert Rosen. Perhaps, if others 
are interested, now would be a time to pursue this. I became aware of 
this book in a conversation with the author of a very interesting, but 
less concise work, _Self-Modifying Systems in biology and cognitive 
science: a new framework for dynamics, information, and complexity_. 
(George Kampis, Oxford: 1991) 

In the winter bulletin of Santa Fe Institute, John Casti wrote "Biology 
and the Loss of Mechanism, or Why the Physicists were Wrong," an 
extremely favorable review of Rosen's book. I do not have easy access to 
a scanner, or i would OCR the review as a prospectus for the virtual 
seminar. [alternatively, perhaps it can be ftp'd direct from SFI].

Rosen's thesis: Physics, committed to the model of formalizable 
mechanism, can not currently understand biology. life is essentially a 
relational process which cannot be captured through a turing machine 
simulation. Rosen's previous book, Anticipatory Systems, laid some of 
the groundwork for this claim.

I am not going to push this thread much further at the moment, but i 
would like to point out that there are millions of interesting questions 
which, while not overthrowing current laws of physics, should make us 
quite interested in the future of science.

This may sound like a suspiciously squishy slide to introduce emergent 
phenomena, then Penrose souls, Searle-sludge, and whatever nimbus 
mesmerizes the current writer. I don't want to stir up all those 
arguments here. 

Ysrael Lettvin [one of the authors, along with McCulloch and Pitts of 
"What the Frog's Eye tells the Frog's Brain"] recently quipped that 
neuroscience turned out to be just "a catalog of transistors with no 
sign of a circuit anywhere." If someone who spent 40 years studying 
neuroscience could say this in public, it does suggest that the current 
paradigm will not exhaust the research opportunities of the future.

As another ex, Tim May claims geography is fairly worked out. LANDSAT 
terabytes are not redundant, if geography becomes interwoven with 
biology (Dorion Sagan made an argument for this linkage in _Biosphere_, 
and SJ Gould lives this link). Instead of a model becoming "refined", it 
becomes rich, thick, and far more complicated.

One other point on the virtual seminar idea:
if we are going to pursue it, i could only process and write on one or 
two chapters per week. that would take five weeks, which is incredibly 
short for an in-depth seminar, but might appear geologically slow to the 
speedy. but most issues that turn out to be of any interest to me 
involve either these questions, or they connect to algorithmic theory of 
information [which is independent, though of high relevance].


