If I may butt in with an observation: I think the idea of a "physics
search space" over-simplifies things a bit. Maybe a better metaphor
would be to consider physics as a fractal? We've iterated over it a
lot, and have a fair idea of the outline -- but we could go on
sharpening the image forever and not pin it down completely. Further:
there exist odd holes and self-similar microcosms within it which, 
although subject to the same principles, may look wildly different 
from anything we've seen before. The development of quantum mechanics
was an example of such a small section being mapped; it obeyed the
same basic principles and was deducable from existing physics, it only
operated within a certain restricted range of conditions, yet it
looked wildly different from 'conventional' physics. A bump in the
coastline ... and I'd be willing to bet that self-similar bumps exist
or will be found in the coastline of the quantum mechanical subdomain.
